1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,125
A brush uncovers a scrap of silver, stamped with marks.

2
00:00:05,125 --> 00:00:07,539
Beside it, a page of palm leaf.

3
00:00:07,539 --> 00:00:08,670
One was money.

4
00:00:08,670 --> 00:00:10,253
One was writing.

5
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:17,026
Neither says who made it.

6
00:00:17,026 --> 00:00:23,522
So here is the question this whole series rests on: how does anyone know what happened three thousand years ago?

7
00:00:30,467 --> 00:00:37,601
There are five ways, and almost everything you will ever read about ancient India comes from one of them.

8
00:00:37,601 --> 00:00:38,812
Things we dug up.

9
00:00:38,812 --> 00:00:40,158
Texts people wrote.

10
00:00:40,158 --> 00:00:42,177
Inscriptions carved in stone.

11
00:00:42,177 --> 00:00:42,648
Coins.

12
00:00:42,648 --> 00:00:46,044
And accounts left by travellers from outside.

13
00:00:51,133 --> 00:00:52,550
Start with digging.

14
00:00:52,550 --> 00:00:58,786
Archaeology gives us what people left behind: pots, tools, beads, houses, bones, seeds.

15
00:00:58,786 --> 00:01:03,054
Its great advantage is that it does not flatter anyone.

16
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:11,198
A broken pot has no opinion.

17
00:01:11,198 --> 00:01:17,117
Its limit is that objects cannot tell you what people believed, or what they called themselves.

18
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:22,826
Then texts.

19
00:01:22,826 --> 00:01:28,398
The Vedas, the epics, Buddhist and Jain writings, Sanskrit and Tamil literature.

20
00:01:28,398 --> 00:01:32,345
These tell us what people thought, which no pot can.

21
00:01:37,767 --> 00:01:43,188
But many were composed long before they were written down, and carried by memory in between.

22
00:01:43,188 --> 00:01:48,152
So historians treat them carefully, and ask when a text took the shape we have.

23
00:01:54,867 --> 00:02:01,253
Inscriptions are the hardest evidence we have, because they were carved at the time, and they were meant to be read.

24
00:02:01,253 --> 00:02:03,572
Ashoka's edicts are the famous ones.

25
00:02:08,567 --> 00:02:16,709
They were unreadable for centuries, until James Prinsep worked out the Brahmi script in 1837 and the stones began to speak.

26
00:02:21,867 --> 00:02:28,447
Coins carry names, titles, dates and images, and they travel, so they show us trade and reach.

27
00:02:28,447 --> 00:02:30,178
And finally, travellers.

28
00:02:30,178 --> 00:02:33,180
Megasthenes came to the Mauryan court.

29
00:02:39,433 --> 00:02:43,472
Fa-Hien came in the Gupta period, Hiuen Tsang in the seventh century.

30
00:02:43,472 --> 00:02:51,388
Outsiders notice what locals never bother to write down — and they also misunderstand things, so their accounts are read with care.

31
00:02:57,533 --> 00:02:58,889
One practical thing.

32
00:02:58,889 --> 00:03:05,712
Places in this series often carry two names: the old one in the sources, and the one on a map today.

33
00:03:10,133 --> 00:03:11,834
Pataliputra is Patna.

34
00:03:11,834 --> 00:03:13,612
Takshashila is Taxila.

35
00:03:13,612 --> 00:03:16,850
The name changes; the place does not.

36
00:03:20,967 --> 00:03:24,617
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

37
00:03:24,617 --> 00:03:26,932
Five sources, five words.

38
00:03:26,932 --> 00:03:29,991
Dig, read, carve, coin, visit.

39
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:37,770
Dig Quiz.

40
00:03:37,770 --> 00:03:39,848
Pause and name the source.

41
00:03:39,848 --> 00:03:44,313
First: letters cut into living rock, by order of a ruler.

42
00:03:44,313 --> 00:03:45,845
An inscription.

43
00:03:50,867 --> 00:03:55,641
Second: a silver piece stamped with marks, carrying no king's name.

44
00:03:55,641 --> 00:03:57,416
A punch-marked coin.

45
00:04:08,267 --> 00:04:13,108
Third: an account of India written by someone who travelled here from abroad.

46
00:04:13,108 --> 00:04:14,536
A traveller's account.

47
00:04:14,536 --> 00:04:21,230
In Mains, a familiar question asks how far literary sources can be relied on for early Indian history.

48
00:04:25,367 --> 00:04:29,177
The honest answer names both the insight and the dating problem.

49
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:40,454
A palm leaf and a coin open the gallery: the two halves of how we know anything at all.

50
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:49,898
Next gallery: the Stone Age, and the first Indians.

51
00:04:49,898 --> 00:04:53,924
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

